# Feature Completeness

## Objective

Deliver the complete, working feature that was requested, including error handling and edge cases — not a happy-path skeleton.

## Core Principles

- No TODO stubs or placeholder functions unless explicitly requested as a scaffold.
- Handle documented edge cases (empty input, not-found, permission denied) not just the ideal case.
- Wire the feature all the way through: UI to API to DB, not just one layer.
- If full completion isn't possible in one pass, say explicitly what's missing rather than presenting it as done.

## Requirements

- Re-check the original request against the delivered code for any dropped requirement.
- Implement loading/error/empty states for UI features, not just the success state.
- Validate inputs at the boundaries (API, forms) that a real user could actually trigger.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Returning a function that only handles the 'happy path' and silently ignores documented edge cases.
- Leaving a `// TODO: handle errors` in code presented as finished.
- Implementing only the frontend or only the backend half of a full-stack request without saying so.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Every explicit requirement from the request maps to actual implemented code.
✓ Edge cases mentioned (explicitly or clearly implied) are handled.
✓ Anything intentionally left out is called out, not hidden.

## Golden Rule

'Done' means it handles real-world inputs, not just the demo case.
